  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 150 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nThe main concerns for the medium-range forecast include severe-\nweather potential on Saturday evening/night, and additional\nchances for storms for much of the following week.\n\nQuasi-zonal midlevel flow will exist in the northern/central\nU.S. this weekend into next week, with faster flow confined to\nthe northern Plains and Upper Midwest, especially this weekend.\nWeak perturbations will migrate eastward in this flow regime,\nbut forcing/lift will be considerably stronger well north of the\narea. This leads to lower predictability with the convective\nforecast this weekend in the lower Missouri Valley, with highly\nvariable output with the convection-allowing models and the\ncoarser deterministic/ensemble guidance as well. Most guidance\ndepicts a weak perturbation ejecting eastward through the\ncentral Plains on Saturday night, leading to convection\ndeveloping in the central Plains (likely to our west) Saturday\nafternoon/evening and progressing eastward into the Missouri\nValley Saturday night into Sunday morning. A weak cold front\nwill be present from the Upper Midwest into the central Plains\non Saturday, with surface convergence possibly sufficient to\ninitiate storms during the afternoon to our west/northwest.\nHowever, large-scale lift will increase Saturday night via the\nlow-level jet and improved warm advection as a lee surface low\ndevelops in the high plains. Given the uncertainties with\nafternoon convection, it is still unclear how storms will \nevolve in our immediate forecast area Saturday night into \nSunday. However, the pattern is certainly conducive for storms \nat some point during the weekend, and the overall environment is\nat least marginally supportive of severe weather (modest shear \nand decent instability, with damaging winds the primary threat).\nThe damaging wind potential may increase if storms Saturday \nevening can organize/congeal into a mesoscale convective system \nand develop a progressive cold pool. The SPC has placed the area\nin a marginal risk for Saturday evening/night, which seems \nreasonable at this stage.\n\nShowers/storms may linger into Sunday across portions of the\narea. Meanwhile, another shortwave trough will eject\nnortheastward from the central Rockies Sunday afternoon/evening\ninto the central Plains. However, downstream ridging will be\nintensifying during this period, so the lift attendant to this\nperturbation may be shunted farther to the north (relative to\nSaturday night). Nevertheless, at least some potential exists\nfor additional storms Sunday night into Monday in proximity to\nthis system. For now, have kept PoPs during this period mostly\nnorth of U.S. Highway 36, though confidence is lower than\naverage. Meanwhile, farther to the south, temperatures will be\nnoticeably warmer by Sunday (mid 80s to lower 90s).\n\nThe large-scale pattern remains similar on Monday (likely\nleading to slightly warmer temperatures overall), but additional\nshortwave troughs will eject northeastward into the\nnorthern/central Plains during this period. With associated\nrounds of convection attendant to these vorticity maxima, heavy\nrainfall becomes an increasing concern with time. At this time,\nthis potential looks highest just to our north (Nebraska and \nIowa); however, with time, this potential will sag southward as \nconvective outflow eventually impinges on the ridging. A \nparticularly potent trough may affect the northern/central U.S. \nby mid to late week, further enhancing the southward trend of \nthe shortwave trough tracks. Pattern recognition suggests that \nheavy rainfall is likely in a favored corridor on the \nsouthern/southeastern fringe of stronger midlevel flow and the \nnorthwestern periphery of the downstream ridging. This may \ninclude some or most of our forecast area, though confidence is \non the low side given the considerable variability apparent in \nensemble output by this portion of the forecast. Nevertheless, \nthe pattern looks increasingly unsettled next week, with \ntemperatures eventually cooling as the stronger midlevel flow \nand associated surface systems/fronts approach the region with \ntime.",
